  "hints": [
    "Run it and check the Queue backlog stat and which component is red.",
    "A queue only moves the problem in time. If producers add 4,800 messages per second and consumers remove 2,000, the backlog grows by 2,800 every second.",
    "Scale the Delivery Worker. 3 replicas is exactly 80% busy, and the objective asks for 75% or less, so use 4."
  ],
  "scoring": {
    "weights": {
      "performance": 0.3,
      "reliability": 0.4,
      "cost": 0.15,
      "architecture": 0.15
    },
    "latencyTargetMs": 50,
    "costBudget": 1100
  },
  "explanation": [
    "Queues decouple latency, not throughput. In steady state, consumer capacity must exceed the producer rate, or the lag grows without bound. Little's Law makes it concrete: wait time = backlog / drain rate.",
    "Monitor consumer lag (messages behind, or age of the oldest message), not just errors. This failure never shows up as an error rate.",
    "Scale consumers horizontally up to the number of queue partitions, and size for peak, not average. A drained queue at 75% utilization can absorb spikes and catch up after them."
  ]
}
